// Pull request author: saakovv
// Change: new rule
// Title: Linux TLS Reverse Shell via OpenSSL
// Sigma ID: b2e9d4f7-8c1a-4e63-b5d2-3f7a1c8e6b40
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Ivan Saakov
// Date: 2026-05-10 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: execution, T1059.004
// Description: Detects execution of openssl with the s_client subcommand combined with -connect and shell
//   indicators. Attackers use openssl to establish encrypted reverse shells that bypass simple
//   cleartext detection of tools like netcat. The technique typically involves mkfifo or /dev/tcp
//   alongside openssl to create a bidirectional encrypted channel to an attacker-controlled
//   listener.
// False positives:
//   - Unlikely in production systems; security testing with explicit authorization
// References:
//   - https://gtfobins.github.io/gtfobins/openssl/
//   - https://www.revshells.com/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/e5bac28da3655c33390db7ef1503db8cd3babb5f/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_openssl_tls_reverse_shell.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "/openssl" and ProcessCommandLine contains "s_client" and (ProcessCommandLine contains "/bin/sh" or ProcessCommandLine contains "/bin/bash" or ProcessCommandLine contains "/bin/zsh" or ProcessCommandLine contains "bash -i" or ProcessCommandLine contains "sh -i")
